@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1735 = private unnamed_addr constant [657 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of the `assert` keyword.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AAssertions are removed when Python is run with optimization requested\0A(i.e., when the `-O` flag is present), which is a common practice in\0Aproduction environments. As such, assertions should not be used for runtime\0Avalidation of user input or to enforce  interface constraints.\0A\0AConsider raising a meaningful error instead of using `assert`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aassert x > 0, \22Expected positive value.\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aif not x > 0:\0A    raise ValueError(\22Expected positive value.\22)\0A\0A# or even better:\0Aif x <= 0:\0A    raise ValueError(\22Expected positive value.\22)\0A```\0A", align 1
