@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1813 = private unnamed_addr constant [1100 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `from collections.abc import Set` imports that do not alias\0A`Set` to `AbstractSet`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `Set` type in `collections.abc` is an abstract base class for set-like types.\0AIt is easily confused with, and not equivalent to, the `set` builtin.\0A\0ATo avoid confusion, `Set` should be aliased to `AbstractSet` when imported. This\0Amakes it clear that the imported type is an abstract base class, and not the\0A`set` builtin.\0A\0A## Example\0A```pyi\0Afrom collections.abc import Set\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```pyi\0Afrom collections.abc import Set as AbstractSet\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe for `Set` imports defined at the\0Atop-level of a `.py` module. Top-level symbols are implicitly exported by the\0Amodule, and so renaming a top-level symbol may break downstream modules\0Athat import it.\0A\0AThe same is not true for `.pyi` files, where imported symbols are only\0Are-exported if they are included in `__all__`, use a \22redundant\22\0A`import foo as foo` alias, or are imported via a `*` import. As such, the\0Afix is marked as safe in more cases for `.pyi` files.\0A", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1814 = private unnamed_addr constant [94 x i8] c"Use `from collections.abc import Set as AbstractSet` to avoid confusion with the `set` builtin", align 1
