@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2051 = private unnamed_addr constant [1819 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `TypeVar` and `ParamSpec` definitions in which the type is\0Aboth covariant and contravariant.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ABy default, Python's generic types are invariant, but can be marked as\0Aeither covariant or contravariant via the `covariant` and `contravariant`\0Akeyword arguments. While the API does allow you to mark a type as both\0Acovariant and contravariant, this is not supported by the type system,\0Aand should be avoided.\0A\0AInstead, change the variance of the type to be either covariant,\0Acontravariant, or invariant. If you want to describe both covariance and\0Acontravariance, consider using two separate type parameters.\0A\0AFor context: an \22invariant\22 generic type only accepts values that exactly\0Amatch the type parameter; for example, `list[Dog]` accepts only `list[Dog]`,\0Anot `list[Animal]` (superclass) or `list[Bulldog]` (subclass). This is\0Athe default behavior for Python's generic types.\0A\0AA \22covariant\22 generic type accepts subclasses of the type parameter; for\0Aexample, `Sequence[Animal]` accepts `Sequence[Dog]`. A \22contravariant\22\0Ageneric type accepts superclasses of the type parameter; for example,\0A`Callable[Dog]` accepts `Callable[Animal]`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom typing import TypeVar\0A\0AT = TypeVar(\22T\22, covariant=True, contravariant=True)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom typing import TypeVar\0A\0AT_co = TypeVar(\22T_co\22, covariant=True)\0AT_contra = TypeVar(\22T_contra\22, contravariant=True)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `typing` \E2\80\94 Support for type hints](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html)\0A- [PEP 483 \E2\80\93 The Theory of Type Hints: Covariance and Contravariance](https://peps.python.org/pep-0483/#covariance-and-contravariance)\0A- [PEP 484 \E2\80\93 Type Hints: Covariance and contravariance](https://peps.python.org/pep-0484/#covariance-and-contravariance)\0A", align 1
