@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2060 = private unnamed_addr constant [875 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `TypeVar`, `TypeVarTuple`, `ParamSpec`, and `NewType`\0Adefinitions in which the name of the type parameter does not match the name\0Aof the variable to which it is assigned.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen defining a `TypeVar` or a related type parameter, Python allows you to\0Aprovide a name for the type parameter. According to [PEP 484], the name\0Aprovided to the `TypeVar` constructor must be equal to the name of the\0Avariable to which it is assigned.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom typing import TypeVar\0A\0AT = TypeVar(\22U\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom typing import TypeVar\0A\0AT = TypeVar(\22T\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `typing` \E2\80\94 Support for type hints](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html)\0A- [PEP 484 \E2\80\93 Type Hints: Generics](https://peps.python.org/pep-0484/#generics)\0A\0A[PEP 484]:https://peps.python.org/pep-0484/#generics\0A", align 1
